One common vulnerability in Java web applications arises from the improper use of the `Runtime.getRuntime().exec()` method when dealing with untrusted user input. This leads to **Command Injection**, where an attacker can append malicious commands to a legitimate input to execute arbitrary code on the server.

```java
import java.io.*;

public class CommandInjectionExample {
    // This method is vulnerable to command injection
    public void executeSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // The userInput is concatenated directly into a shell command
        // An attacker could provide: "image.jpg; rm -rf /"
        String command = "ls -l " + userInput; 

        // The process executes the string as a raw shell command
        Runtime.getRuntime().exec(command);
    }
}
***
import java.io.*;
import java.util.List;

public class SafeCommandExample {
    // This method is fixed and uses ProcessBuilder to prevent injection
    public void executeSystemCommand(String userInput) throws IOException {
        // Instead of a single string, we use a List of arguments.
        // This treats the userInput strictly as a single literal argument,
        // preventing shell metacharacters (like ; or &) from being interpreted.
        ProcessBuilder processBuilder = new ProcessBuilder("ls", "-l", userInput);

        // Start the process safely
        processBuilder.start();
    }
}
```

